  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1235 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nToday - Friday: Warmer & Breezy Today, Staying Dry \n\nNorthwesterly synoptic flow continues to dominate the synoptic flow \npattern over the next few days with shortwave ridging moving through \nthe region for the daytime hours. As a result, flow turns to \nsoutherly out ahead of a surface front that will push through the \nregion later into the day. Consequently, a tightening surface \npressure gradient and daytime diurnal mixing will aid in\nincreasing surface winds to around 15-20 mph with gusts \nreaching to around 30 mph, mainly west of the Mississippi River.\nWith this southerly push bringing 850mb warm air advection into\nthe region, temperatures respond accordingly with the inter-\nquartile range of the NBM keeping highs in the lower to upper \n70s across much of the area with some potential for areas near \nI-35 to approach 80 degrees in the higher percentiles. \nEventually a surface front pushes through the region later into \nthe evening, resulting in surface winds to shift to \nnorthwesterly and thus bring in cooler temperatures. Overall, \ngiven the lack of robust convergence along this front, thinking \nany precipitation is unlikely which is further suggested in the \nCAMs.\n\nAs we head later into the work week, northwesterly flow persists \nwith minimal modification to the airmass overhead. Consequently, \ntemperatures remain relatively steady-state behind the frontal \npassage on Tuesday evening with highs generally in the middle 60s to \nlower 70s. With minimal synoptic forcing present across much of \nguidance in this northwesterly flow regime, thinking precipitation \nchances will be kept to a minimum which is supported in the grand \nensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) which has minimal (under 20% \nchance) measurable precipitation chances through Friday. \n\nThis Weekend: Slight Warm Up\n\nBy the time the weekend rolls around, northwesterly flow gives way \nto zonal flow aloft as an upper-level ridge moves into the region. \nConsequently, this will allow for southerly flow to push into the \nregion with increasing warm air advection. As a result, the grand \nensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) generally agrees on \npushing high temperatures further above normal into the weekend \nwith the inter-quartile spread for high temperatures on Sunday \nranges from the lower 70s to near 80 degrees. With upper-level \nridging overhead during this timeframe, thinking that \nprecipitation chances are unlikely through Sunday. However, \nthere is some indication for the ridge to break down by Sunday \nnight and into early next week and allow for some stronger \nsynoptic forcing to work its way into the region. This could \nlead to an increase in precipitation chances very late into the \nweekend into early next week. However, predictability remains \nfairly low at this stage as to when this ridge breaks down so \nthinking the modest (20-40%) precipitation chances in the NBM \nare reasonable.",
